<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Via Aemilia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">187 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Via Emilia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Via Aemilia was a Roman road built in 187 BCE by the consul Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, running in a straight line along the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> from Ariminum (Rimini) to Placentia (Piacenza) on the Po. Towns along it, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, Reggio and Parma, grew into the medieval communes of the region, which still bears its name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-aemilia.jpg" alt="Via Aemilia" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MacMoreno · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VIII_-_AEMILIA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>To the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> of the republican period northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Gallia Cisalpina</a>, inhabited by Celtic tribes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> who had colonised it in the 4th and 5th centuries BCE. Its great plain, the Pianura Padana, held the best agricultural land of the peninsula and offered the Romans room for mass colonisation. They subdued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> in hard campaigns in the late 3rd century BCE, and the Via Flaminia was completed by 220 BCE. Hannibal's invasion between 218 and 203 BCE overturned Roman control, and tribes such as the Insubres and the Boii rebelled and joined him. Only in 189 BCE were the rebels pacified enough for work on the Via Aemilia to begin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Roman method was to build a road straight through newly conquered land and then plant colonies of civilians or veterans along it, on plots confiscated from the defeated peoples. The Via Aemilia followed this plan: Bononia was founded in 189 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Mutina</a>, Regium and Parma in 183 BCE. The consul Marcus Aemilius Lepidus completed it in 187 BCE and it was named after him. It ran largely in a straight line for 176 Roman miles, about 260 km, north-west from Rimini to Piacenza, partly on elevated embankments and partly over an older road system. It crossed many tributaries of the Po, among them the Rubicon near Rimini and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebbia</a> near Piacenza, and joined the Via Flaminia at Rimini.</p><p>Piacenza became the key road hub of the plain: the Via Postumia linked it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> in 148 BCE, and in 109 BCE the consul Marcus Aemilius Scaurus completed the Via Aemilia Scaura to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>. Augustus rebuilt the road in 2 BCE from Rimini to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebbia">Trebbia</a>, as a milestone found in the bed of the Reno records.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The road launched intensive Roman colonisation of the plain, which became the most populous and economically important part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. By the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a>, Romanisation was so complete that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Gallia Cisalpina</a> was abolished and absorbed into Italia. The road gave its name to the region, already called Aemilia by the time Augustus took sole power; around 7 BCE, when he divided Italia into eleven regions, the eighth was first named Padus and later Aemilia.</p><p>Several Roman bridges remain, among them the Ponte di Tiberio at Rimini, begun by Augustus and completed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, which still carries its two dedicatory inscriptions. The modern State Road 9 is still officially called Via Emilia and in many places lies directly over the Roman road. Its inhabitants are today called Emiliani.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Via Aemilia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Parma and Piacenza, two prosperous cities of the Po plain on the <b>Via Emilia</b>, had belonged to the Duchy of Milan for much of the late Middle Ages before Pope Julius II annexed them to the Papal States in 1512.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of Mutina, troops loyal to Mark Antony, after initial success, were defeated on the <b>Via Aemilia</b> by legions of the Roman Senate under the consuls Gaius Pansa and Aulus Hirtius.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It controlled the <b>Via Emilia</b> and a route across the Apennines to Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-parma">Commune of Parma</a></li><li><blockquote>Piacenza, an old Roman colony where the <b>Via Emilia</b> meets the Po, had consuls by 1126.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-piacenza">Commune of Piacenza</a></li><li><blockquote>Reggio, on the <b>Via Emilia</b>, had consuls by 1136, after the citizens took power from the bishop and from the lords of the Canossa lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-reggio">Commune of Reggio</a></li></ul></section>
